What:           /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/acquisition_health
Date:           September 2026
Contact:        PlutoSDR experimental receiver contributors
Description:
                Read-only fresh PSMA acquisition health, including when no map
                bank is ready. ASCII format is "PSMH 1 46" followed by exactly
                46 whitespace-separated, eight-digit lowercase hexadecimal
                unsigned 32-bit words and a newline. Word pairs are low/high.

                  0       admitted FPGA ABI version
                  1       declared source rate in MSPS, not measured PHY rate
                  2       fresh coherent hardware snapshot generation
                  3..4    live hardware status before/after receipt
                  5       driver streaming/enabled/IRQ-live bits 0/1/2
                  6       driver fault bitmask
                  7..9    driver maps/chunks delivered, buffer push failures
                  10      coherent ready-bank mask
                  11..12  coherent map generation for banks 0/1
                  13..16  coherent bank 0/1 start index, low/high per bank
                  17      coherent discarded count
                  18      coherent discontinuity count
                  19      coherent map overrun count
                  20      coherent protocol error count
                  21      coherent arithmetic overflow count
                  22..23  coherent map read/release error counts
                  24      coherent hardware health flags
                  25      coherent ingress dropped count
                  26..28  coherent scheduler gap/index/overflow counts
                  29      coherent detector fault count
                  30      coherent phase discontinuity count
                  31..32  live bridge read/release errors
                  33      live snapshot-request overrun count
                  34      live DDC telemetry mode (see below)
                  35..38  live DDC accepted/emitted counts, low/high per count
                  39..40  live DDC discontinuity/saturation counts
                  41..43  coherent accepted/published/zero-denominator counts
                  44..45  coherent ingress/candidate FIFO levels

                FIFO words encode current level in low16 and high water in
                high16. Unpaired counters retain their existing u32 widths;
                do not interpret them as unbounded observation totals. Known
                diagnostic health bits and zero-denominator events are not
                equivalent to fatal acquisition faults. Decode using word0's
                explicitly admitted hardware contract.

                Words 2, 10..30 and 41..45 describe ONE hardware snapshot.
                All other words are separately sampled observations. The read
                holds the same driver mutex as IRQ delivery and lifecycle
                operations; this does not make live hardware counters atomic
                with each other or with the snapshot.

                DDC mode0 is absent for 15 MSPS (ABIs 1.1/1.5), with words
                35..40 zero. Mode1 exposes live low32 only for ABIs 1.2/1.3;
                words36/38 are zero, not measured high words. Mode2 (ABI1.4)
                reads each 64-bit counter with bounded high/low/high retries.
                The mode2 accepted/emitted pair is still not mutually atomic.

                Reading requests a new snapshot but does not clear any fault,
                release map banks, flush/stop acquisition, or alter IRQ state.
                A successful read can contain faults; success is not a healthy
                verdict. Busy snapshots, timeout, generation races, exhausted
                snapshot counters, changed FPGA identity/rate, or repeatedly
                torn live counter reads return an error instead of a receipt.

                Users must bind receipts to an owned serial, boot, session,
                source interval and measured PHY configuration, retain failed
                and late-fault evidence, and require fresh generations. A
                fault-free receipt alone proves neither all DMA/IIO/disk bytes
                arrived nor RF validity, GLRT detection or FPGA timing lock.
